## Extracting Text from a Document using JavaScript

The following JavaScript function extracts the text from a given HTML document. It utilizes the DOM to traverse the document and extract the text content.

### Function Implementation

```javascript
/**
 * Extracts the text from a given HTML document.
 * 
 * @param {Document} document The HTML document to extract text from.
 * @returns {string} The extracted text.
 */
function extractTextFromDocument(document) {
    // Remove script and style elements as they don't contain relevant text
    const scriptsAndStyles = document.querySelectorAll('script, style');
    scriptsAndStyles.forEach(element => element.remove());

    // Get the text content of the document body
    const text = document.body.textContent;

    // Remove extra whitespace and return the result
    return text.trim().replace(/\s+/g, ' ');
}

// Example usage:
const exampleHtml = `
<html>
    <head>
        <title>Example Document</title>
    </head>
    <body>
        <h1>Hello, World!</h1>
        <p>This is an example document.</p>
        <script>alert('This script will be removed');</script>
        <style>body { background-color: #f2f2f2; }</style>
    </body>
</html>
`;

// Create a new DOM parser to parse the example HTML
const parser = new DOMParser();
const doc = parser.parseFromString(exampleHtml, 'text/html');

// Call the function to extract the text
const extractedText = extractTextFromDocument(doc);

console.log(extractedText);
// Output: "Hello, World! This is an example document."
```

### How it Works

1. The function takes a `Document` object as an argument.
2. It removes all `<script>` and `<style>` elements from the document, as they typically don't contain relevant text content.
3. It retrieves the text content of the document's `<body>` using the `textContent` property.
4. The extracted text is then processed to remove extra whitespace characters, resulting in a cleaner output.
5. Finally, the function returns the extracted and processed text.

### Notes

* This function is designed to work in a browser environment where the `Document` object is available. If you're working in a Node.js environment, you'll need to use a library like `jsdom` to create a `Document` object from an HTML string.
* The function removes `<script>` and `<style>` elements to avoid including their content in the extracted text. If you need to preserve these elements, you can modify the function accordingly.

Here is a Java method that uses Java Stream to check if a list has not more than one entry and returns the single entry if it exists, or null if the list is empty, and throws an exception if there are more than one entries.

```java
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Optional;

public class Main {

    /**
     * Returns the single element from the list if it exists, 
     * null if the list is empty, and throws an exception if there are more than one entries.
     * 
     * @param list the list to check
     * @param <T> the type of elements in the list
     * @return the single element from the list, or null if the list is empty
     * @throws IllegalStateException if the list contains more than one element
     */
    public static <T> T getSingleEntryOrNull(List<T> list) {
        return list.stream()
                .limit(2) // Limit the stream to 2 elements to avoid unnecessary processing
                .reduce((a, b) -> { 
                    // If there's more than one element, throw an exception
                    throw new IllegalStateException("More than one entry in the list"); 
                })
                .orElse(null); // Return null if the list is empty
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> listWithOneEntry = List.of("one");
        List<String> listWithMultipleEntries = List.of("one", "two");
        List<String> emptyList = List.of();

        System.out.println(getSingleEntryOrNull(listWithOneEntry)); // prints: one
        try {
            System.out.println(getSingleEntryOrNull(listWithMultipleEntries));
        } catch (IllegalStateException e) {
            System.out.println(e.getMessage()); // prints: More than one entry in the list
        }
        System.out.println(getSingleEntryOrNull(emptyList)); // prints: null
    }
}
```

This code defines a method `getSingleEntryOrNull` that takes a list as an argument and uses Java Stream to process it. The `limit(2)` call is used to avoid unnecessary processing if the list is large and has more than one element. The `reduce` operation is used to check if there's more than one element in the stream. If there is, it throws an `IllegalStateException`. If there's at most one element, `reduce` returns an `Optional` containing that element, or an empty `Optional` if the list is empty. The `orElse(null)` call then returns the element if it exists, or `null` if the list is empty.

The `main` method demonstrates the usage of `getSingleEntryOrNull` with different lists.

Here's an implementation of a Kalman filter in GNU Octave. This example assumes a simple linear system with a state transition model and a measurement model. The state transition model predicts the state at the next time step based on the current state, and the measurement model relates the state to the measurements.

```octave
function [x_est, P_est] = kalman_filter(A, B, H, Q, R, x_init, P_init, measurements, controls)
  % Initialize variables
  num_steps = size(measurements, 1);
  num_states = size(x_init, 1);
  x_est = zeros(num_states, num_steps);
  P_est = zeros(num_states, num_states, num_steps);

  % Set initial conditions
  x_est(:, 1) = x_init;
  P_est(:, :, 1) = P_init;

  for k = 2:num_steps
    % Prediction step
    x_pred = A * x_est(:, k-1) + B * controls(:, k-1);
    P_pred = A * P_est(:, :, k-1) * A' + Q;

    % Innovation
    innovation = measurements(:, k) - H * x_pred;
    S = H * P_pred * H' + R;

    % Kalman gain
    K = P_pred * H' / S;

    % Update step
    x_est(:, k) = x_pred + K * innovation;
    P_est(:, :, k) = (eye(num_states) - K * H) * P_pred;
  endfor
endfunction
```

### Example Usage

Let's consider a simple example where we're tracking the position and velocity of an object moving in one dimension. The state vector `x` is `[position; velocity]`. The system dynamics are defined by a constant velocity model.

```octave
% System matrices
A = [1, 1; 0, 1];  % State transition matrix
B = [0; 0];        % Control input matrix (no control inputs in this example)
H = [1, 0];        % Measurement matrix (measuring position)
Q = [0.01, 0; 0, 0.01];  % Process noise covariance
R = 1;             % Measurement noise variance

% Initial conditions
x_init = [0; 0];   % Initial state estimate
P_init = [1, 0; 0, 1];  % Initial state covariance

% Generate some measurements
num_steps = 100;
measurements = zeros(1, num_steps);
controls = zeros(size(B, 2), num_steps);
true_state = zeros(2, num_steps);
true_state(:, 1) = [0; 1];  % Initial true state

for k = 2:num_steps
  true_state(:, k) = A * true_state(:, k-1) + randn(2, 1) .* sqrt(diag(Q));
  measurements(k) = H * true_state(:, k) + randn(1) * sqrt(R);
endfor

% Run the Kalman filter
[x_est, P_est] = kalman_filter(A, B, H, Q, R, x_init, P_init, measurements, controls);

% Plot results
figure;
plot(true_state(1, :), 'g', 'linewidth', 2); hold on;
plot(x_est(1, :), 'r', 'linewidth', 2);
plot(measurements, '.k');
legend('True Position', 'Estimated Position', 'Measurements');
xlabel('Time Step');
ylabel('Position');
```

This code defines a simple linear system, generates some noisy measurements, and then applies the Kalman filter to estimate the state. The results are plotted, showing the true position, the estimated position, and the measurements.

To implement a reverb effect on microphone input using Kotlin and JNI (Java Native Interface), you will need to interact with the Android NDK (Native Development Kit) to access native audio APIs. The Android NDK provides the OpenSL ES API for high-performance audio processing. Here's a basic guide on how to achieve this. This example will outline the steps to capture audio from the microphone, apply a simple reverb effect, and then play it back.

### Step 1: Setting Up Your Project

1. **Install the Android NDK**: Ensure you have the Android NDK installed in your Android Studio. You can do this by going to `SDK Manager` > `SDK Tools` and checking `NDK (Side by side)` and `CMake`.

2. **Create a new Android project**: Create a new project in Android Studio. When creating the project, make sure to select "Include C++ support" to enable native code development.

### Step 2: Implementing the Native Code

1. **OpenSL ES for Audio Processing**: You'll use OpenSL ES for capturing and playing back audio.

2. **Create a Native Method for Initializing Audio**: In your `native-lib.cpp`, you'll define functions for initializing the audio capture and playback, and for applying the reverb effect.

Here's a simplified version of `native-lib.cpp`:

```cpp
#include <jni.h>
#include <SLES/OpenSLES.h>
#include <SLES/OpenSLES_Android.h>

// Engine and output mix
SLObjectItf engineObject = NULL;
SLEngineItf engineEngine = NULL;
SLObjectItf outputMixObject = NULL;

// Recorder and player
SLObjectItf recorderObject = NULL;
SLObjectItf playerObject = NULL;
SLRecordItf recorderRecord = NULL;
SLPlayItf playerPlay = NULL;

// Buffer queue
SLAndroidSimpleBufferQueueItf recorderBufferQueue = NULL;
SLAndroidSimpleBufferQueueItf playerBufferQueue = NULL;

// Reverb effect
SLObjectItf reverbObject = NULL;
SLEnvironmentalReverbItf reverbItf = NULL;

extern "C" {
    JNIEXPORT void JNICALL
    Java_com_example_reverb_MainActivity_initAudio(JNIEnv *env, jobject instance) {
        // Create engine
        slCreateEngine(&engineObject, 0, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL);
        (*engineObject)->Realize(engineObject, SL_BOOLEAN_FALSE);
        (*engineObject)->GetInterface(engineObject, SL_IID_ENGINE, &engineEngine);

        // Create output mix
        const SLInterfaceID ids[1] = {SL_IID_ENVIRONMENTALREVERB};
        const SLboolean req[1] = {SL_BOOLEAN_TRUE};
        (*engineEngine)->CreateOutputMix(engineEngine, &outputMixObject, 1, ids, req);
        (*outputMixObject)->Realize(outputMixObject, SL_BOOLEAN_FALSE);

        // Create reverb effect
        (*engineEngine)->CreateEnvironmentalReverb(engineEngine, &reverbObject, ids, req);
        (*reverbObject)->Realize(reverbObject, SL_BOOLEAN_FALSE);
        (*reverbObject)->GetInterface(reverbObject, SL_IID_ENVIRONMENTALREVERB, &reverbItf);

        // Configure reverb settings
        SLEnvironmentalReverbSettings reverbSettings = SL_I3DL2_ENVIRONMENT_PRESET_STONECORRIDOR;
        (*reverbItf)->SetEnvironmentalReverbProperties(reverbItf, &reverbSettings);

        // Create audio recorder and player
        // For simplicity, the detailed implementation of creating recorder and player is omitted.
        // You need to implement the code for recording and playing back audio.

        // Set reverb effect on the output mix
        (*outputMixObject)->GetInterface(outputMixObject, SL_IID_ENVIRONMENTALREVERB, &reverbItf);
    }

    JNIEXPORT void JNICALL
    Java_com_example_reverb_MainActivity_startRecording(JNIEnv *env, jobject instance) {
        // Start recording
        if (recorderRecord != NULL) {
            (*recorderRecord)->SetRecordState(recorderRecord, SL_RECORDSTATE_RECORDING);
        }
    }

    JNIEXPORT void JNICALL
    Java_com_example_reverb_MainActivity_stopRecording(JNIEnv *env, jobject instance) {
        // Stop recording
        if (recorderRecord != NULL) {
            (*recorderRecord)->SetRecordState(recorderRecord, SL_RECORDSTATE_STOPPED);
        }
    }

    JNIEXPORT void JNICALL
    Java_com_example_reverb_MainActivity_startPlayback(JNIEnv *env, jobject instance) {
        // Start playback
        if (playerPlay != NULL) {
            (*playerPlay)->SetPlayState(playerPlay, SL_PLAYSTATE_PLAYING);
        }
    }

    JNIEXPORT void JNICALL
    Java_com_example_reverb_MainActivity_stopPlayback(JNIEnv *env, jobject instance) {
        // Stop playback
        if (playerPlay != NULL) {
            (*playerPlay)->SetPlayState(playerPlay, SL_PLAYSTATE_STOPPED);
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Kotlin Code to Call Native Methods

In your `MainActivity.kt`, you will call the native methods to initialize, start, and stop the audio processing.

```kotlin
import androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity
