To create a compound datatype for the given struct `Data` using the HighFive library (which is a modern C++ interface for HDF5), you need to define a compound datatype that matches the layout of your struct. The `Data` struct contains an array of 3 doubles and an integer.

Here's a step-by-step example on how to achieve this:

1. **Define your struct**: You already have this defined as `struct Data { double v[3]; int n; };`.

2. **Create a HighFive compound datatype**: You will use `HighFive::DataType::create` to define the compound datatype. This involves specifying the types and names of the members of your struct.

Here's how you can do it:

```cpp
#include <HighFive/H5DataType.hpp>
#include <HighFive/H5DataSet.hpp>
#include <HighFive/H5File.hpp>

struct Data {
    double v[3];
    int n;
};

int main() {
    // Create a compound datatype
    HighFive::CompoundType datatype = HighFive::DataType::create<Data>();

    // Alternatively, you can manually specify it if auto creation doesn't work as expected
    // or if you need more control
    // HighFive::CompoundType datatype = HighFive::CompoundType({
    //     {"v", HighFive::DataType::FromArray<double>(3)},
    //     {"n", HighFive::DataType::FromType<int>()}
    // });

    // To use the datatype, you would typically create a dataset or attribute with it.
    HighFive::File file("example.h5", HighFive::File::ReadWrite | HighFive::File::Create | HighFive::File::Truncate);

    // Create a dataset with the compound datatype
    Data data = {{1.0, 2.0, 3.0}, 4};
    HighFive::DataSet dataset = file.createDataSet("/data", HighFive::DataSpace::From(data), datatype);
    dataset.write(data);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, `HighFive::DataType::create<Data>()` automatically generates a compound datatype based on the layout of `Data`. This is the recommended way if your struct is simple and standard-layout.

However, if the automatic creation doesn't work as expected (for example, due to padding issues or complex struct layouts), you can manually define the compound datatype as shown in the commented-out section. You specify each member of the compound datatype with its name and type. For the array `double v[3]`, we use `HighFive::DataType::FromArray<double>(3)` to represent an array of 3 doubles.

Make sure your struct is standard-layout (a requirement for `HighFive::DataType::create` to work correctly), meaning it doesn't have any virtual functions, doesn't inherit from a non-standard-layout class, and all non-static data members are at the same access level (all public, all private, etc.), among other requirements defined by the C++ standard.

