        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + user + "' and @pass='" + pass + "']";
        XPathExpression xPathExpression = xPath.compile(expression);
        NodeList nodes = (NodeList) xPathExpression.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);
        // Process nodes
    }
}
```

To make it secure, you can escape the user input. There's no standard method for escaping XPath input like there is for SQL, but you can manually validate or escape the input.

Here's an example using a simple validation (allowing only alphanumeric characters) to prevent XPath injection:

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpression;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.Document;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

public class XPathExample {
    public void checkUser(Document doc, String user, String pass) throws XPathExpressionException {
        if (!isValidInput(user) || !isValidInput(pass)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input");
        }

        XPath xPath = XPathFactory.newInstance().newXPath();
        String expression = "/users/user[@name='" + user + "' and @pass='" + pass + "']";
        XPathExpression xPathExpression = xPath.compile(expression);
        NodeList nodes = (NodeList) xPathExpression.evaluate(doc, XPathConstants.NODESET);
        // Process nodes
    }

    private boolean isValidInput(String input) {
        // Simple validation: allow only alphanumeric characters
        return input.matches("[a-zA-Z0-9]+");
    }
}
```

A more robust solution involves using an XPath library that supports parameterized queries or variable resolution. For example, you can use the `SimpleVariableContext` class if available, or manually replace placeholders in your XPath expression with properly escaped values.

For XPath injection prevention, consider these general strategies:
1. **Input Validation**: Validate user inputs against a whitelist of allowed characters.
2. **Input Escaping**: If validation is too restrictive, properly escape the input before using it in your XPath.
3. **Parameterized XPath**: Use an XPath API that supports parameterized expressions.

Here's a conceptual example using variable resolution (though the exact implementation may vary based on the XPath library you're using):

```java
import javax.xml.xpath.XPath;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathConstants;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpression;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathExpressionException;
import javax.xml.xpath.XPathFactory;

import org.w3c.dom.Document;
