**Sending a POST Request with a Client Certificate in C#**

Below is a C# program that sends a POST request with a client certificate attached.

### Prerequisites

* .NET Core 3.1 or later
* A client certificate in PFX format

### Code

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Security.Cryptography.X509Certificates;
using System.Text;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace ClientCertificatePostRequest
{
    class Program
    {
        static async Task Main(string[] args)
        {
            // URL of the server to send the request to
            string url = "https://example.com/api/endpoint";

            // Path to the client certificate PFX file
            string certificatePath = "path/to/client-certificate.pfx";

            // Password for the client certificate PFX file
            string certificatePassword = "certificate-password";

            // Data to be sent in the request body
            string requestBody = "{\"key\":\"value\"}";

            try
            {
                // Load the client certificate
                X509Certificate2 certificate = new X509Certificate2(certificatePath, certificatePassword);

                // Create a new HttpClientHandler with the client certificate
                var handler = new HttpClientHandler();
                handler.ClientCertificates.Add(certificate);

                // Create a new HttpClient instance
                using var client = new HttpClient(handler);

                // Set the request content
                var content = new StringContent(requestBody, Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");

                // Send the POST request
                var response = await client.PostAsync(url, content);

                // Check the response status code
                if (response.IsSuccessStatusCode)
                {
                    Console.WriteLine("Request sent successfully.");
                    Console.WriteLine(await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync());
                }
                else
                {
                    Console.WriteLine($"Request failed with status code {response.StatusCode}.");
                }
            }
            catch (Exception ex)
            {
                Console.WriteLine($"An error occurred: {ex.Message}");
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation

1. Load the client certificate from a PFX file using `X509Certificate2`.
2. Create an `HttpClientHandler` instance and add the client certificate to its `ClientCertificates` collection.
3. Create an `HttpClient` instance using the `HttpClientHandler`.
4. Set the request content using `StringContent`.
5. Send the POST request using `PostAsync`.
6. Check the response status code and read the response content if successful.

### Example Use Case

Replace the placeholders (`url`, `certificatePath`, `certificatePassword`, and `requestBody`) with your actual values to send a POST request with a client certificate. Ensure that the client certificate is valid and trusted by the server.

To extract hashtags from text in C#, you can use regular expressions. The idea is to match any word that starts with `#` followed by one or more alphanumeric characters or underscores. Here's a simple example of how you can achieve this:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

public class HashtagExtractor
{
    private static readonly Regex HashtagRegex = new Regex(@"#\w+", RegexOptions.Compiled);

    public static string[] ExtractHashtags(string text)
    {
        var matches = HashtagRegex.Matches(text);
        var hashtags = new string[matches.Count];
        for (int i = 0; i < matches.Count; i++)
        {
            hashtags[i] = matches[i].Value;
        }
        return hashtags;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        string text = "This is a sample #text with #hashtags like #CSharp.";
        string[] hashtags = ExtractHashtags(text);
        Console.WriteLine("Extracted Hashtags:");
        foreach (var hashtag in hashtags)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(hashtag);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:

1. We define a regular expression `#"#\w+"` that matches any string starting with `#` followed by one or more (`+`) word characters (`\w`). Word characters include letters (both uppercase and lowercase), digits, and underscores.

2. The `ExtractHashtags` method takes a string as input and uses the `HashtagRegex` to find all matches.

3. The matches are then copied into an array of strings, which is returned by the method.

4. In the `Main` method, we demonstrate how to use `ExtractHashtags` with a sample text and print out the extracted hashtags.

This code is case-sensitive and considers `#CSharp` and `#csharp` as different hashtags. If you want to normalize the case of the extracted hashtags, you could modify the `ExtractHashtags` method to convert them to lower case, for example:

```csharp
hashtags[i] = matches[i].Value.ToLower();
```

This way, all extracted hashtags will be in lower case, making it easier to compare or count them later on.

